## Is eczema a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. A long-term skin condition that affects everyday activities, such as using your hands, sleeping or getting dressed, can be covered. The law also treats a **severe disfigurement** as having a substantial effect, even if it does not affect what you can do.

## Dermatitis caused by work

Jobs with lots of **wet work**, such as hairdressing, cleaning, catering and healthcare, or contact with **chemicals**, can cause or worsen hand eczema (contact dermatitis). Your employer must assess and reduce the risk, for example with suitable **gloves** and skin care. Tell your GP and employer. If you were an employee and developed dermatitis from substances at work, you may be able to claim **Industrial Injuries Disablement Benefit**.

## Reasonable adjustments

- suitable gloves and hand care products
- changes to tasks to reduce wet work
- a cooler workspace, and uniform in soft fabrics
- time off for appointments and treatments such as phototherapy

## Benefits

Most people with eczema will not qualify for PIP. Severe eczema may affect activities such as **preparing food**, **washing** and **dressing**, if your hands are cracked and bleeding, or **managing treatments**, if you need help to apply creams or wet wraps.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

For children with severe eczema who need a lot of extra care, read about [DLA for children](/benefits/dla/).
